WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.060
<v Instructor>So let's go ahead and create a database.</v>

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.370
So I'm in the Aurora and RDS console.

3
00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.260
On the left-hand side, I click on Databases,

4
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.383
and then I click on Create database.

5
00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.740
And as we can see here, we have different options.

6
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.860
So it can be a full configuration or an easy create.

7
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.690
Because we want to see the different options,

8
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.490
we're going to go over full configuration.

9
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.710
Here you can select the engine options.

10
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.950
So you have Aurora, either MySQL or PostgreSQL-compatible.

11
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:26.997
Then you have MySQL, PostgreSQL,

12
00:00:26.997 --> 00:00:28.860
and other types of engines.

13
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:29.730
For this hands-on,

14
00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.730
we're going to select MySQL to keep it simple.

15
00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.310
Then you scroll down, and then for engine version,

16
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.760
just choose whatever default there is right here.

17
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.650
Next, for templates, we can have Production,

18
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.720
which gives us access to more settings,

19
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.000
or Dev/Test, or Free tier.

20
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.640
So Free tier is going to be what we're gonna be choosing.

21
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.530
But if you take the Production setting,

22
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.960
what's going to happen is that you have the option

23
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.130
to have Multi-AZ DB instance deployment with two instances,

24
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.440
or even Multi-AZ DB cluster deployment

25
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.060
with three instances.

26
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.380
So these are production.

27
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.430
We don't need this right now,

28
00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:05.220
but it gives you an idea of the kind of setup you can have

29
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.083
across multiple availability zones.

30
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.270
But we're going to use Free tier.

31
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.430
And as you can see, the only thing we can use right now

32
00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:14.910
is Single-AZ database instance deployment, one instance.

33
00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.620
Alright, perfect.

34
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.453
Let's scroll down.

35
00:01:17.453 --> 00:01:20.610
We keep everything as default for database identifier.

36
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.050
Master username, yes.

37
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.550
And then credentials management.

38
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.240
So here we have the option to either create our password

39
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.150
and then have RDS manage the password,

40
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.150
or we can also have the password managed directly

41
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.880
by AWS Secrets Manager.

42
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:37.110
So this is the most secure,

43
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.560
but there is a cost to using AWS Secrets Manager.

44
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.410
So we won't use this; we'll use self-managed,

45
00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.750
but you should know that it's an option anyway.

46
00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.720
So for the master password, I will just enter password,

47
00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.850
and I will confirm the master password.

48
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:52.203
It's very weak, I know.

49
00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.340
Then we'll just enable only password authentication.

50
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.100
But as you can see, you have the option to also have IAM

51
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.053
to authenticate to the database.

52
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.800
Then you scroll down, and for instance configuration,

53
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.920
we have information around, well, what's free.

54
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.440
So we'll use the db.t4g.micro,

55
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.120
maybe you have a db.t3.micro,

56
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.200
maybe you have something else.

57
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.033
It doesn't matter.

58
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.710
Just choose whatever the default is right now.

59
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.080
For storage type, we'll have 20 gigabytes.

60
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:19.980
But then as you can see,

61
00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.080
under additional storage configuration,

62
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.510
there is the option to enable storage autoscaling.

63
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.910
And that means that if you use all the storage,

64
00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.440
you can autoscale up to, for example,

65
00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.503
1000 gigabytes of storage.

66
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:33.750
For connectivity,

67
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.110
we'll select Don't connect to an EC2 compute resource.

68
00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:38.460
We'll select the default VPC,

69
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.290
and we don't change anything.

70
00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.780
For subnet group, no, we don't.

71
00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:43.613
We keep it as default.

72
00:02:43.613 --> 00:02:45.150
And for public access, we'll say yes,

73
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.140
because we want to be able

74
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.803
to publicly access our RDS database with a public IP.

75
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.740
For the VPC security group, we will choose Create new,

76
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.590
and I'll call this one demo-rds.

77
00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.800
And then we don't have any preference for the AZ.

78
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.750
We don't want an RDS proxy.

79
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.670
And for additional configuration, we're good to go.

80
00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.410
We'll use port 3306.

81
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.300
That is the port that we're going to connect

82
00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.940
to on our MySQL database.

83
00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.110
Let's scroll down.

84
00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.260
We choose standard insights from monitoring.

85
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.180
As you can see, we can enable enhanced monitoring

86
00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.390
and also export the logs if we wanted to.

87
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.190
And then for the additional configuration

88
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.850
under database initial name, enter mydb.

89
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:28.410
For estimated monthly cost,

90
00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.020
it gives you information around the RDS Free Tier,

91
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.700
and this is available for 12 months

92
00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:34.320
for these instance types.

93
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.153
So we're good to go.

94
00:03:35.153 --> 00:03:37.023
Now let's click on Create database.

95
00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.530
So while my database is being created,

96
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:44.460
let's go ahead and download SQL Electron.

97
00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.680
And this is a SQL client that we're going to use

98
00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.840
to connect to our database.

99
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.673
So very simple.

100
00:03:49.673 --> 00:03:52.080
You're going to click on Download GUI,

101
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:53.760
and then click on it.

102
00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.100
Take the latest version for whatever platform you have.

103
00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.950
So you have latest Windows,

104
00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.350
and then for Mac, it's going to be a DMG.

105
00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:06.350
So I'm gonna do this one, then open it and install it.

106
00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.850
Okay, so my database is now created.

107
00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.140
It took a little bit of time,

108
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.837
and now, as you can see, it is available.

109
00:04:12.837 --> 00:04:15.150
And so what I'm going to show you are a few options.

110
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.760
So first of all, we have the endpoint right here

111
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.680
and the port 3306.

112
00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.110
And it's linked to this security group.

113
00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.070
And this security group,

114
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.590
if you click on it and look at the inbound rules,

115
00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.720
we can see that we have an inbound rule here

116
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:29.880
on TCP port 3306.

117
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.260
So that's great, okay?

118
00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.960
Coming from just my IP address.

119
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.040
Okay, so you should of course modify this security group

120
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.650
if you have problems connecting to your database instance

121
00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.600
to maybe allow it from anywhere (IPv4).

122
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.450
Okay.

123
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.820
So now let's take this endpoint

124
00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.700
and we're going to connect to it using SQL Electron.

125
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.580
So using SQL Electron, I'm going to add a new database.

126
00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.753
I'll call it RDSDemo.

127
00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.770
It's a database of type MySQL.

128
00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.930
And then for the server address, I'm going to enter

129
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.330
what I have right here on port 3306.

130
00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.380
The user is admin, and the password for me was password.

131
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.710
And then the initial database is myDB

132
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.420
as I entered it from before.

133
00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.990
Let's click on Test, and the connection is successful.

134
00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:17.820
If it's not successful, please have a look

135
00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.650
whether or not your database was set

136
00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.450
to be a public database, number one,

137
00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.850
and number two, have a look at your security group settings

138
00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.760
to make sure that your IP is allowed in.

139
00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.610
So let's save this and connect to our database.

140
00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:31.320
And now I'm connected into my database,

141
00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.760
directly into myDB,

142
00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.650
which was the database that was created.

143
00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.820
And in here, I could go ahead

144
00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.640
and enter some CREATE TABLE statements.

145
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:43.560
So CREATE TABLE mytable,

146
00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.427
and then just a name of VARCHAR(20),

147
00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.133
and a first_name of VARCHAR(20).

148
00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.740
So it's just a very quick statement

149
00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:57.480
just to show you how SQL works,

150
00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.420
but this is definitely out of scope for the exam.

151
00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.253
Execute this.

152
00:06:01.253 --> 00:06:03.810
So now we have my_table that has been created,

153
00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:06.390
and then we don't have any content in it, okay?

154
00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.730
But you could have an INSERT statement

155
00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.130
to insert some data into your table.

156
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.020
So you would say, for example,

157
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.397
enter the value ('marrek'),

158
00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:18.420
and then the value here ('Stephane').

159
00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:19.680
Execute this.

160
00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.870
And then if you click on Select rows,

161
00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:24.420
then you see that my_table has my name and my first name

162
00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.860
to be marrek and Stephane.

163
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.610
So this is how we would use an RDS database with MySQL.

164
00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:30.960
But again, out of scope of the exam,

165
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.400
I just want to show you the full picture

166
00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.830
of using SQL Electron.

167
00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.040
So back in here, my RDS database is fully managed,

168
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:38.873
so this is great.

169
00:06:38.873 --> 00:06:39.706
We can use it.

170
00:06:39.706 --> 00:06:40.539
This is amazing.

171
00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:42.660
Other things we can do that are worthy are

172
00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.360
to create a read replica directly from this database.

173
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.550
So we can create a read replica,

174
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:49.510
and then this read replica would allow us

175
00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:53.070
to have more read capacity for our database,

176
00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.600
which would be very, very helpful.

177
00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:58.080
And do we want whether or not that database to be Multi-AZ

178
00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.640
for recovery purposes?

179
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.563
So I'll keep it as no and cancel it.

180
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.310
Next, we can have a look at monitoring.

181
00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.400
And monitoring, for example, has the CPU Utilization.

182
00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.233
And as we can see,

183
00:07:09.233 --> 00:07:11.490
while the CPU Utilization is monitored by my database,

184
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.080
and we can monitor this, for example,

185
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.370
for scaling our database

186
00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:17.280
if in case we have too many connections into it.

187
00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.410
We can have a look at the database connection count as well

188
00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:23.040
to see how many clients are connected into our database.

189
00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.560
And a lot of metrics overall, okay?

190
00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.660
Other things you can do with RDS

191
00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.160
is around creating snapshots.

192
00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.690
So you can take a snapshot,

193
00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.160
and this will snapshot your database,

194
00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.960
and you can restore it wherever you want.

195
00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:36.600
You can restore it to a point in time for your database,

196
00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.370
or you can even migrate a snapshot

197
00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.430
if you wanted to migrate it into a different region,

198
00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.270
for example.

199
00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:44.460
So all these are a bunch of options that we can use.

200
00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.350
But as we can see, the whole power of RDS is

201
00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.720
to manage databases for us.

202
00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.060
To have replicas, Multi-AZ,

203
00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:52.980
you know, we can increase the instance type

204
00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.390
as we go along, and so on.

205
00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.860
And that all comes very handy

206
00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.660
from a managed service perspective.

207
00:07:57.660 --> 00:07:59.070
So you've seen all you need to know

208
00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:00.570
for your RDS database.

209
00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:01.530
I hope you liked it.

210
00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.880
And just to finish yourself,

211
00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.240
please make sure to delete the database.

212
00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:06.390
So to delete it,

213
00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:10.230
first you need to remove the termination protection.

214
00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:11.943
So you go to Modify,

215
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.540
and then at the very bottom, you will scroll down

216
00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.970
and find the delete protection setting.

217
00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:19.500
So here it is.

218
00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.410
We'll disable it, press Continue,

219
00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.540
and then Apply immediately.

220
00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.660
And then once the delete protection is done,

221
00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.730
you can go ahead and delete this database.

222
00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.490
You can remove creating a final snapshot

223
00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.080
and then say delete me,

224
00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.060
and acknowledge the fact that upon deletion,

225
00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.350
everything will be lost.

226
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.020
Click on Delete, and you're good to go.

227
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:40.890
So that's it for this lecture.

228
00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:42.060
I hope you liked it,

229
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.010
and I will see you in the next lecture.

